From 9b1b87b6ef30fc745574dcb2b21333d7efe29ae1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 03:36:30 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 364/365] DOS-PORT: patch 0364 -- lever 2: memoise
 GetCharIDAtRoom inside a whitelisted engine call

MMD's scripts ask the same character hit test several times a tick with nothing in between that can
change the answer: 4 lookups a tick inside the VM on character select, 3 of them repeats; each scans
all 102 characters (~0.41 ms/tick of the script event on the 486, MMD-REMAINDER). A 4-entry memo
keyed (x, y, hit_options) and a generation counter answers a repeat, restoring char_lowest_yp too.

When it may be used: only while the innermost engine call made by the fast VM is on a 10-name
whitelist -- the calls MMD makes between a store and a hit, each audited to change nothing the hit
test reads and to run no tick or script: GetLocationType, GetLocationName, StrGetCharAt, StrLen,
StrSetCharAt, StrCat, StrCopy, GetPlayerCharacter, GetCursorMode, GetInvAt. What invalidates (bumps
the generation): any other engine call (object, plugin and unknown calls included); a generic VM
step other than a memory read or a write into the script's own memory (its stack, a global
variable, its global data block); the line hook; the pending-events check; every VM entry and exit;
a legacy Run(). The hooks are lazy -- they test anything only while the memo holds a live entry --
and out of line, so the fast loop keeps its register allocation (a first version with locals cost
+0.32 ms/tick at fixed cycles; an inline stepper test cost the loop its DosVmStep inlining, hence
DosVmStep/DosVmStepBody). Engine-side hit tests (outside the VM) are never memoised.

Lab-only (-DDOSAGS_VMTRACE, DOSAGS_ENGLEV_SHADOW=1): every hit is recomputed and compared, result
and char_lowest_yp ("cmemo MISMATCH"); statewit gains cm=. Lab: 0 mismatches over 13,872 hits (MMD
character select 2997, intro 10875); digests EQUAL on the four fixtures; aq-journeys 10/10.
486, round L5 (lab binary 23fd25201ec9, VM fast both arms, levers 1+2 off vs on): MMD displayed
91.28 / 91.24% -> 99.90 / 99.90% at 40.00 ticks/s (B-A +8.62 / +8.66, predicted +7 to +10.5).

